  <title>7.8.4: Writing Equivalent Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01874</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.4: Writing Equivalent Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>afe84971-31fd-4ff9-91df-ca78cc330f61</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Draw a diagram to show that \((3x+1)(x+2)\) is equivalent to \(3x^2+7x+2\).</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para>Diagram should show partial products of \(3x(x)\), \(6x\), \(x\), and 2, which add up to \(3x^2+7x+2\).</para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00001">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_09_01.png"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Is \((x+4)^2\) equivalent to \(2x^2+8x+8\)? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No. \((x+4)(x+4)=x^2+4x+4x+16=x^2+8x+16\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Why Should I Care?</title>
        <figure id="fig-00002">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_09_02.png"/>
          </media>
        
        <para>Anna's class entered a contest where they had to build a catapault and use it to launch a pumpkin at a distant target. But when Anna's class shot their pumpkin for the first time, it went wildly off course.</para>
        <para>Anna thought about their problem and came up with a solution. If Anna's class used a quadratic equation, they could mathematically find the best place and angle for their pumpkin catapault. It worked! Their accuracy went from 2% to 99%.</para>
        <para>You can use quadratic equations for any object that travels in a curved path, like footballs, frisbees, and golf balls.</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
